# Self-Absorption Analysis in LIBS

## 1. Background

Self-absorption is an important phenomenon in laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS). It occurs when emitted radiation from excited species is reabsorbed by lower-energy atoms or ions of the same species along the optical path.

This process can modify the observed emission line intensity and profile, potentially affecting quantitative elemental analysis.

## 2. Relevance to the Research

The MSc research investigated self-absorption effects in laser-induced plasma generated from Cu–Al2O3 composite materials.

Particular attention was given to the behavior of characteristic copper and aluminum emission lines and the influence of self-absorption on spectroscopic measurements.

## 3. Analytical Considerations

The spectral analysis considered:

- Emission line identification using reference atomic data.
- Relative emission intensity and spectral line behavior.
- Signal-to-noise ratio of identified emission features.
- The influence of self-absorption on quantitative LIBS measurements.

## 4. Research Objective

The objective was to investigate and account for self-absorption effects to improve the reliability of compositional analysis in LIBS measurements of industrial composite materials.

## 5. Thesis Context

This analysis formed part of the MSc thesis:

"Laser-Induced Micro-Plasma Spectroscopy for Quality Control of Industrial Composites with Self-Absorption Correction."

